    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Here Are Some of the Serious and Obscure Brands That EICMA 2026 Had To Offer – Worth Knowing About\n1968 Plymouth Road Runner Packs Numbers Matching 426 Hemi Muscle, Documented From Day One\n37 photos\nPhoto: Mecum (2017)\nWhen Plymouth introduced the Road Runner for the 1968 model year, the mission was simpler than an Acme anvil drop: strip away luxury fluff, bolt in big-block Mopar muscle, and undercut every rival on price. Nearly six decades later, top-tier survivors command serious collector money, and this 1968 Plymouth Road Runner 426 Hemi automatic coupe proves why. Documented with its original window sticker, broadcast sheet, and numbers-matching drivetrain, it embodies pure cartoon street violence.\nPlymouth paid Warner Bros. $50,000 to license the elusive desert bird and spent another $10,000 developing a signature horn loud enough to give Wile E. Coyote a migraine. The rookie model entered a brutal 1968 showroom brawl against the premiere of the second-generation Dodge Charger and second-generation Pontiac GTO, the debut of its corporate twin, the Dodge Coronet Super Bee, and Plymouth's newly restyled, upscale GTX, which had debuted in 1967.\nBuilt on the 116-inch-wheelbase Belvedere B-body platform during its 1968 to 1970 first generation—within a 13-year run lasting through 1980—the 1968 Road Runner offered taxicab austerity and zero small-block excuses. Early buyers could order only a two-door pillared coupe with pop-out rear windows before a two-door hardtop joined mid-year.\nPower came strictly from a 335-hp 383 Road Runner four-barrel V8 with 440 heads (exclusive to the new muscle car), or the 425-hp 426 Hemi , mated to a 4-speed manual or 3-speed TorqueFlite automatic.\nPhoto: Mecum (2017) Executives hoped to move 20,000 units in 1968, yet buyers stampeded showrooms for 44,599 cars worldwide—29,240 pillared coupes and 15,359 hardtops—including 44,303 U.S. deliveries. The standard 383-cubic-inch V8 powered 43,294 U.S. examples, leaving bias-ply tires whimpering across America.\nThat 383 tally split into 28,138 pillared coupes—comprising 13,707 equipped with the 4-speed manual and 14,431 with the TorqueFlite automatic—and 15,156 hardtops, divided between 6,686 with the 4-speed manual and 8,470 with the automatic.\nRemarkably, 1968 was the only year one nameplate sold over 1,000 Hemi cars, with 1,009 U.S.-spec Road Runners and 1,019 worldwide. That 1,009-car run comprised 840 coupes—449 of which were 4-speeds and 391 automatics, including 9 for Canada—and 169 hardtops, split into 108 4-speeds and 61 automatics.\nPhoto: Mecum (2017) Overall, Chrysler built 2,439 Hemi Mopars in 1968: 1,019 Road Runners, 468 Dodge Charger R/Ts, 446 Plymouth GTXs, 229 Dodge Coronet R/Ts, 125 Dodge Super Bees, 2 standard Coronets, 80 Race Hemi Dodge Darts, and 70 Plymouth Barracudas (the last two strictly as drag-strip toys).\nOne St. Louis-built pillared coupe had a scheduled build date of November 28, 1967, shipping new to Lloyd Motors Inc. in Lake Geneva, Wisconsin. Its original window sticker lists a $2,870 base price—which bundled heavy-duty springs, shocks, brakes, torsion bars, and a sway bar—before the buyer checked the 426 Hemi at $714.30, a surcharge steep enough to fund a crate of Acme dynamite.\nThe order sheet also added black vinyl roof for $81.60, TorqueFlite automatic for $38.95, Sure-Grip differential for $42.35, Solid State AM radio for $61.55, rear seat speaker for $14.05, buffed paint for $21.95, and Red Streak tires. After a $3,844.75 subtotal and a $52.00 destination charge, the final MSRP landed at $3,896.75. Which was anything but cheap .\nPhoto: Mecum (2017) This coupe wears factory AA1 Silver Metallic paint beneath its black vinyl top. Aside from modest “Hemi” hood and trunk badges, a blacked-out horizontal-blade grille, and standing “Road Runner” decals, the pillared coupe looks deceptively polite—like a Sunday church commuter waiting to drop a two-ton boulder on an unsuspecting rival at the next traffic light.\nInside, the cabin pairs black upper door frames with a silver-and-black vinyl split-bench seat that looks like a tuxedo crossbred with a taxicab. Skipping bucket seats and a center console, the original buyer kept the column shifter for the 3-speed automatic, leaving room for three across the front when outrunning hungry coyotes. A column-mounted 8,000-rpm tachometer, 150-mph horizontal speedometer, AM radio, and cartoon horn button complete the cockpit.\nBeneath the twin-bulge hood sits the numbers-matching 426-cubic-inch Street Hemi V8, factory-rated at 425 hp at 5,000 rpm and 490 lb-ft of torque at 4,000 rpm. In the video attached to this story (posted in 2017 by a selling dealer from that time), the host notes it produced over 500 hp at 6,000 rpm.\nPhoto: Mecum (2017) Currently breathing through a pair of slightly larger modern Carter AFB four-barrel carburetors for extra airflow, the Hemi Orange powerhouse comes with its original numbers-matching Carter AFBs and original cast-iron Prestolite distributor preserved in boxes.\nUnderneath, the restored chassis retains its original rust-free floor pans, quarter-panel drop-downs, and Hemi torque boxes. The A727 TorqueFlite automatic feeds an 8.75-inch Sure-Grip rear axle with 3.23:1 gears, supported by asymmetrical Hemi leaf springs, a heavy-duty front sway bar, and 11x3-inch front and 11x2.5-inch rear drum brakes. Fresh shocks, lines, and a 2.5-inch H-pipe exhaust with stainless mufflers and 2.25-inch chrome-tipped tailpipes round out the hardware.\nSpread across 59 years, the car's paper trail could make a librarian weep. Tracked across four owners from day one—including the original buyer, who bought it back in 2005—the coupe starred in the 1996 Mopar calendar and carries a Galen V. Govier Chrysler Registry report ranking it number 11 of 42 accounted for among 382 U.S. Hemi automatic coupes, alongside vintage 35-millimeter family photos, the Certicard, and the spring-stained broadcast sheet.\nPhoto: Mecum (2017) After hammering for $67,000 at Mecum Kissimmee in 2017 ($73,700 final price) and listing through Hanksters Hot Rods for $84,900 with 76,976 miles, this coupe is now offered by Ohio Corvettes and Muscle Cars in North Canton, Ohio, showing 77,890 miles, for $139,500.\nWhile the 12-month median price across all 1968 Road Runners sits at $55,995—and around $122,000 for Hemi cars—this museum-documented Hemi Mopar time capsule only needs a single Acme mail-order gadget to be perfect: a check signed by a new owner.\nVia // Ohio Corvettes and Muscle Cars\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHemi Mopar Road Runner Plymouth Road Runner Plymouth Mopar muscle car b-body mopar 1968 Plymouth Road Runner\nAbout the author: Razvan Calin\nAfter nearly two decades in news television, Răzvan turned to a different medium. He’s been a field journalist, a TV producer, and a seafarer but found that he feels right at home among petrolheads. Full profile\nUP NEXT Ford Starts Making CATL-Licensed LFP Battery Cells in Michigan for the 2028 Fathom EV",
